National Stock Exchange of India (NSE) shares fell below their initial public offering (IPO) price on Monday amid profit booking in the stock, days after its market debut.
The stock hit a low of Rs 1,761 per share, down more than 1 percent from the previous close. The fall came as Indian equity markets declined sharply, with investors remaining cautious amid rising oil prices following a stalemate in US-Iran peace talks.
 NSE shares had settled nearly 2 percent higher on their market debut on September 24, valuing the exchange at Rs 4.49 lakh crore and placing it among the world's top seven exchanges.
The stock listed at Rs 1,800, a 0.84 percent premium over its IPO price of Rs 1,785 on the BSE. During the session, it rose 5.21 percent to Rs 1,878 before closing at Rs 1,818, up 1.85 percent over the issue price.
NSE had a market valuation of Rs 4,49,955 crore at the end of its debut session. Its valuation stood at Rs 4,37,580 crore on Monday, a decline of Rs 12,375 crore.
The Rs 22,569-crore IPO of NSE, the country's second-largest public issue, was subscribed 5.71 times, with strong demand from institutional investors. The issue received bids for 50.58 crore shares against 8.86 crore shares on offer.
The exchange had fixed a price band of Rs 1,700-1,785 per equity share for the IPO, with the offer price set at Rs 1,785 per share.
Analysts, meanwhile, expect upside in the stock, with brokerages setting price targets implying gains of up to 15 percent from the IPO issue price.
PL Capital initiated coverage on NSE with an 'Accumulate' rating and a price target of Rs 1,950 per share, implying an upside of about 9.2 percent over the issue price of Rs 1,785.
