Indian equity benchmarks on Monday fell to their lowest level in six months, after seven straight weeks of losses, as rising crude oil prices, geopolitical tensions and weak global cues weighed on sentiment.
Foreign fund outflows also weighed on investor sentiment.
The Sensex settled 1,124.02 points or 1.52 percent lower at 72,771.72, while the broader Nifty declined to 22,791, down 349.50 points or 1.5 percent, its lowest level since April 2.
The Nifty and Sensex have declined for seven consecutive weeks, losing nearly 6 percent, in one of their longest weekly losing streaks on record.
All 16 major sectors were trading in the red. The broader Nifty Smallcap 100 and Nifty Midcap 100 indices slipped 1.43 percent each.
 1) Rise in crude oil prices : Brent crude, the global oil benchmark, traded 2.27 percent higher at USD 106.7 per barrel after US President Donald Trump said he had rejected an Iranian proposal to reopen the Strait of Hormuz and end fighting. Iran, however, insisted on Sunday that only diplomacy can resolve its conflict with the United States and Israel, reported Reuters.
For India, the world's third-largest crude importer, higher oil prices risk stoking inflation, widening the import bill and squeezing corporate margins.
"Higher oil prices, rising inflation and lack of adequate liquidity remain cause of major concerns for domestic markets," G Chokkalingam, founder and head of research at Equinomics Research, told Reuters, adding "the short-term outlook for Indian markets remain quite pessimistic."
 2) Weak global cues : In Asian markets, South Korea's KOSPI, Japan's Nikkei 225 index and Shanghai's SSE Composite index traded lower. Wall Street futures traded up to 0.07 percent lower, indicating a weak start for US equities.
 3) FII selling : Foreign Institutional Investors (FIIs) extended their selling for a second consecutive session on Friday, offloading equities worth Rs 3,696 crore.
 4) Rupee declines : The rupee depreciated 20 paise to 95.95 against the US dollar as the greenback strengthened further and foreign fund outflows continued amid the ongoing West Asia conflict.
At the interbank foreign exchange market, the rupee opened at 95.89 against the American currency and lost further ground to touch 95.95, registering a loss of 20 paise from its previous closing level.
 5) Profit booking : Profit booking emerged at higher levels as investors locked in gains after markets rose in the previous session.
On Friday, the Sensex climbed 315.20 points, or 0.43 percent to settle at 73,895.74. The Nifty advanced 77.40 points, or 0.34 percent to end at 23,140.50.
 6) Decline in financials : High-weightage financials and banks lost 1.6 percent each. Shares of top private lender HDFC Bank slid 1.8 percent, while ICICI Bank dropped 1.7 percent.
 7) Rise in India Vix : The fear gauge, or volatility index, rose 14 percent to 13.86. A rise in the India VIX indicates higher expectations of market volatility and reflects increased caution among investors.
Among stocks, bucking the broader trend, Allied Blenders & Distillers gained 2 percent after Nomura initiated coverage with a "buy" rating and a price target of Rs 850, implying an upside of 18.9 percent.
Ponmudi R, CEO of Enrich Money, said "Nifty closed sharply lower after facing sustained selling pressure throughout the session. Although the index attempted to stabilize around the crucial 22,800 zone and staged a brief recovery, the rebound failed to sustain, with the index eventually slipping below this level and touching an intraday low of 22,762. On the upside, 22,900–23,000 is likely to act as the immediate resistance zone, with 23,000 remaining a crucial level. Only a sustained move above 23,000 could help stabilize the technical structure and prevent further deterioration."
"On the downside, 22,700 remains an important support level. A sustained break below 22,700 could intensify selling pressure and expose the index to lower levels, with 22,500 emerging as the next key support zone. Momentum indicators remain weak, with the RSI around 28, indicating deeply oversold conditions, while the MACD continues to remain in negative territory, confirming persistent downside momentum," he added.
