Amid continued selling pressure and a break below crucial support levels, experts advised caution. According to them, the indices could see a bounce after becoming oversold, although the sustainability of any such gains remains uncertain. The Nifty 50 needs to hold support at 22,750, near Monday’s low, for a move towards 23,000. However, a decisive break below this level could drag the index towards 22,500. Meanwhile, if the Bank Nifty fails to defend the 54,400 level, near Monday’s low, further weakness towards 54,000-53,800 cannot be ruled out. On the other hand, a sustained move above 54,400 could push the index towards 55,000.
On September 28, the Nifty 50 plunged 360 points, or 1.56 percent, to 22,780, while the Bank Nifty fell 1,109 points, or 1.99 percent, to 54,472. Market breadth was dominated by bears, with 2,582 shares declining against 694 advancing shares on the NSE.
 Jay Thakkar, Vice President & Head of Derivatives and Quant Research at ICICI Securities 
Even after having fallen for the last 7 consecutive weeks, the Nifty 50 continued to fall on Monday indicates that the selling pressure is high. The index broke the critical support of 23,000 quite swiftly and below that level there was no pause as the next stop is at 22,500 level which has the next highest Put base.
On the upside, until 23,000 level is not reclaimed back, there is no chance of any recovery, hence the range has shifted lower to 22,500-23,000 levels.
The FII net index short positions continue to rise and it has now crossed 3.10 lakh contracts which is highly oversold, however, there is no reversal in the index. The PCR is not yet oversold for this monthly settlement as it is at 0.62 level and for the next series too it is not much oversold as yet, hence until 23,000 level is not taken off on the upside, the short-term trend remains sideways to negative.
 Strategy : Sell Nifty Futures with stop-loss above 23,000 and target of 22,500 and 22,200.
 Jigar S Patel, Senior Manager - Equity Research at Anand Rathi 
Nifty has decisively breached the major 22,800 support zone and closed below this level, indicating continued pressure in the near term. However, the momentum indicators are showing an interesting divergence. On September 15, when Nifty registered a lower level, the RSI was at 22.23, indicating deeply oversold conditions. Despite the recent lower lows in price, the current RSI reading has improved to 27.69. This suggests that while price action remains weak, downside momentum may be losing some intensity.
Additionally, India VIX is trading at elevated levels, reflecting heightened market volatility and risk aversion. Therefore, a sharp recovery or short-covering bounce could emerge over the coming few sessions. Traders should closely monitor RSI, volatility and price action around the recent lows for confirmation of a potential reversal.
 Strategy : Buy Nifty Futures with a stop-loss of 22,600, and target of 23,250.
 Somil Mehta, Head of Retail Research at Mirae Asset ShareKhan 
